nio: block the boot thread unconditionally so the init AST can run

thread_yield_internal(1) first asks SCHED(thread_should_yield)(); if the
scheduler does not think another thread is worth running it returns without
giving the CPU up.  On this board the init thread is on the runq with the
processor RUNNING but thread_select never picks it (runs 645/683), so the
yield was a no-op and the init thread bsd_ast never ran.

Call thread_block_reason(AST_YIELD) directly, the same primitive the kernel
uses for its own yield (syscall_subr.c:329).  This skips the should_yield
test and physically blocks the boot thread, handing the CPU to the init
thread.  Diagnostic, one line, no probe marks.

diff --git a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
--- a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
@@ -1284,4 +1284,4 @@ bsd_init(void)
 	 * never returns, the init thread is running bsdinit_task and launchd,
 	 * which is the goal. */
-	thread_yield_internal(1);
+	thread_block_reason(THREAD_CONTINUE_NULL, NULL, AST_YIELD);
 }
